Chapter: TrustVouchDepth
  cites Trust chapter TrustLattice
  cites Foreword chapter Hamt
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 BACKLOG 6.4. The vouch walk is memoised, and this pins the one thing the
 memo must get right: it is keyed on the agent AND THE DEPTH.

 An agent's score is not a property of the agent. The walk is capped at
 depth 5, so the score depends on how much depth budget is left when the
 agent is reached: at depth 5 `best-vouch-score` asks at depth 6, the cap
 refuses, and the score collapses to `direct-score`. The same agent at
 depth 1 gets the rest of the walk.

 `y` below is exactly that agent, and it is reachable two ways:

   x <- y <- z            y at depth 1, z at depth 2: y scores 8000
   x <- c1 <- c2 <- c3 <- c4 <- y    y at depth 5: y scores 0

 y has no direct score, so at depth 5 -- where it cannot reach z -- it is
 worth nothing, and at depth 1 it is worth 8000. Both are correct answers
 to different questions.

 The long path is deliberately vouch index 0, so it is walked FIRST. A
 memo keyed on the bare agent name would therefore cache `y = 0` from the
 depth-5 arrival, and the depth-1 arrival would read that 0 back and score
 x at 0 instead of 6400. The first answer would poison the real one, and
 the failure is silent: a trust score is just a number, and a wrong one
 admits or refuses a quotation with no diagnostic anywhere.

 That is the shape "cache scores on the lattice" would take if the key
 were the agent alone. This test is what stands between that and a
 quotation gate that trusts the wrong work.

 Measured, not assumed: with `memo-key` cut down to the bare agent, x
 reads 0 here instead of 6400 -- and every other trust test in the tree
 still passes, `trust-lattice-test` included. This file is the only thing
 that fails, which is the whole reason it exists.

 The arithmetic, so the number is not a mystery. decay is
 voucher * strength * 8000 / 100000000, every strength here is 10000:
   z direct                       = 10000
   y at depth 1 = decay(z, 10000) = 8000
   x           = decay(y, 10000)  = 6400
 and down the long path decay(0, 10000) = 0 at every rung, so it
 contributes nothing and `max` keeps 6400.

Section: Build

 Vouch order is insertion order, so c1 is index 0 and is walked first.

  graph : TrustLattice
  graph =
    let l0 = lattice-add-direct lattice-empty "z" 10000
    in let l1 = lattice-add-vouch l0 "z" "y" 10000
    in let l2 = lattice-add-vouch l1 "c1" "x" 10000
    in let l3 = lattice-add-vouch l2 "y" "x" 10000
    in let l4 = lattice-add-vouch l3 "c2" "c1" 10000
    in let l5 = lattice-add-vouch l4 "c3" "c2" 10000
    in let l6 = lattice-add-vouch l5 "c4" "c3" 10000
    in lattice-add-vouch l6 "y" "c4" 10000

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("z: " & show (lattice-trust-score graph "z"))
    print-line-uni ("y: " & show (lattice-trust-score graph "y"))
    print-line-uni ("x: " & show (lattice-trust-score graph "x"))
  end
